WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.00
This is journey thirteen of Hewn, the software factory: follow an increment to

00:00:06.00 --> 00:00:06.35
merge.

00:00:06.35 --> 00:00:09.99
Stage ten builds a product one increment at a time, test first,

00:00:09.99 --> 00:00:11.78
and merges each one on its own.

00:00:11.78 --> 00:00:15.47
Owners and Viewers want to watch that happen: the failing tests,

00:00:15.47 --> 00:00:19.86
each attempt to make them pass, the pull request, its checks, and the merge.

00:00:19.86 --> 00:00:24.19
This video walks through how it was built: the progress the worker reports,

00:00:24.19 --> 00:00:28.64
stored checks, a signed webhook, a poller, the increment page, and the tests.

00:00:29.79 --> 00:00:34.42
A user opens an increment and sees red recorded before any implementation,

00:00:34.42 --> 00:00:36.81
or a flag when the tests passed first.

00:00:36.81 --> 00:00:39.75
They see each iteration against the cap of ten,

00:00:39.75 --> 00:00:44.14
and an increment that never goes green blocked with its failing tests,

00:00:44.14 --> 00:00:45.39
while Hewn moves on.

00:00:45.39 --> 00:00:50.03
Once the pull request is open, its link and its required checks show live,

00:00:50.03 --> 00:00:51.03
within a second.

00:00:51.03 --> 00:00:55.04
And when every check passes, it squash-merges, with no approval.

00:00:56.19 --> 00:00:57.35
Here is the flow.

00:00:57.35 --> 00:01:01.75
The worker opens the pull request through the API and reports it.

00:01:01.75 --> 00:01:07.38
Checks reach Hewn three ways: the worker's own reads, a signed webhook from GitHub,

00:01:07.38 --> 00:01:08.26
and a poller.

00:01:08.26 --> 00:01:11.71
Each change is stored, emits a check changed event,

00:01:11.71 --> 00:01:15.03
and the increment page reads the increment again.

00:01:16.18 --> 00:01:21.26
Stage ten already ran test first, then implement until green, then the video,

00:01:21.26 --> 00:01:23.30
the pull request and the merge.

00:01:23.30 --> 00:01:25.94
What it didn't do was say so as it went.

00:01:25.94 --> 00:01:31.27
Now the worker reports the test count with red, the iteration as each one starts,

00:01:31.27 --> 00:01:36.48
the iteration that went green, the video as recorded or failed with its reason,

00:01:36.48 --> 00:01:39.64
and the pull request number the moment it opens.

00:01:39.64 --> 00:01:43.20
The API stamps the time of each step on the increment,

00:01:43.20 --> 00:01:47.75
and redacts the failing tests and the video note before storing them.

00:01:48.90 --> 00:01:54.18
Required checks are now stored, one row per check, per pull request, per increment.

00:01:54.18 --> 00:01:58.69
The check recorder takes the project's lock, updates only what changed,

00:01:58.69 --> 00:02:04.10
and appends a check changed event for each change, under the project's next sequence,

00:02:04.10 --> 00:02:05.56
before broadcasting it.

00:02:05.56 --> 00:02:09.25
The worker's own check reads go through the same recorder,

00:02:09.25 --> 00:02:11.66
so the page sees what the worker sees.

00:02:12.81 --> 00:02:16.51
GitHub can tell Hewn about a check as soon as it changes.

00:02:16.51 --> 00:02:20.20
The webhook is anonymous, so the signature is everything.

00:02:20.20 --> 00:02:25.51
Hewn computes the H-MAC SHA two fifty six of the raw body with the webhook secret,

00:02:25.51 --> 00:02:29.01
and compares it with GitHub's header in constant time.

00:02:29.01 --> 00:02:33.10
No secret configured, no header, a malformed one or a mismatch,

00:02:33.10 --> 00:02:37.44
and the answer is four oh one, logged with the delivery, the event,

00:02:37.44 --> 00:02:40.23
the address and the reason, never the body.

00:02:41.38 --> 00:02:46.40
Webhooks don't always arrive: a self-hosted Hewn may sit behind a firewall.

00:02:46.40 --> 00:02:52.02
So a poller looks every ten seconds for open pull requests that have been silent for

00:02:52.02 --> 00:02:54.03
sixty, and reads their checks.

00:02:54.03 --> 00:02:58.38
Any news, from a webhook, the worker or a poll, resets the clock,

00:02:58.38 --> 00:03:02.13
so a pull request with working webhooks is never polled.

00:03:03.28 --> 00:03:07.28
In Mission Control, each Backlog title now opens its increment.

00:03:07.28 --> 00:03:11.16
The page follows the mock: a test first timeline on the left,

00:03:11.16 --> 00:03:13.32
and the pull request on the right.

00:03:13.32 --> 00:03:16.56
The timeline builds each step from what was stored.

00:03:16.56 --> 00:03:19.73
Red recorded shows the failing and passing counts.

00:03:19.73 --> 00:03:22.91
Tests that passed first are flagged, never hidden.

00:03:22.91 --> 00:03:28.24
And a blocked increment names its failing tests, and the increment Hewn moved on to.

00:03:28.59 --> 00:03:34.06
The pull request card shows its title, size and state, then the required checks,

00:03:34.06 --> 00:03:38.24
each with its status and duration, read out as well as drawn,

00:03:38.24 --> 00:03:40.22
and announced as they change.

00:03:40.22 --> 00:03:42.55
Then the body, section by section.

00:03:42.55 --> 00:03:46.51
If GitHub can't be read, the page says so, with try again.

00:03:47.66 --> 00:03:52.27
The backend tests came first, and failed with not found and unauthorized.

00:03:52.27 --> 00:03:57.14
Now eight tests cover red, each iteration and green, tests that passed first,

00:03:57.14 --> 00:04:00.24
a blocked increment and the one Hewn moved on to,

00:04:00.24 --> 00:04:03.65
the pull request with its checks and the squash-merge,

00:04:03.65 --> 00:04:08.51
a signed webhook emitting check changed, unsigned and wrongly signed webhooks

00:04:08.51 --> 00:04:11.73
refused, the poller after sixty seconds of silence,

00:04:11.73 --> 00:04:13.95
and Viewers following an increment.

00:04:14.30 --> 00:04:18.09
All green, the backend has two hundred and fourteen tests,

00:04:18.09 --> 00:04:23.12
the worker one hundred and forty four, and the frontend eight new view tests.

00:04:23.12 --> 00:04:27.70
End to end, there are nineteen new tests: seven for the page's states,

00:04:27.70 --> 00:04:31.95
six visual comparisons with the mocks, five accessibility checks,

00:04:31.95 --> 00:04:33.72
and one with a real worker.

00:04:33.72 --> 00:04:38.76
To make checks visible, the fake GitHub can now take a few seconds per check.

00:04:38.76 --> 00:04:43.53
The live test watches red, green, the checks turning green and the merge,

00:04:43.53 --> 00:04:47.85
all without a reload, then sends a signed and an unsigned webhook.

00:04:49.00 --> 00:04:53.71
Here is Kiln Booking's first increment, opened as stage ten takes it.

00:04:53.71 --> 00:04:56.65
The tests are written, and red is recorded.

00:04:56.65 --> 00:05:01.65
The implementation goes green after one iteration, the video is recorded,

00:05:01.65 --> 00:05:03.49
and the pull request opens.

00:05:03.49 --> 00:05:09.03
The three required checks turn green one by one, and the increment squash-merges,

00:05:09.03 --> 00:05:10.67
with its branch deleted.

00:05:11.02 --> 00:05:14.27
The merged increment shows every step with its time.

00:05:14.27 --> 00:05:16.59
It works in dark mode and on a phone.

00:05:16.94 --> 00:05:21.09
And here is Parcel Post, whose main workflow never went green.

00:05:21.09 --> 00:05:24.98
It is blocked after ten iterations, with its failing test,

00:05:24.98 --> 00:05:27.52
and the pull request was never opened.

00:05:28.67 --> 00:05:30.67
Journey thirteen is complete.

00:05:30.67 --> 00:05:34.39
Red is recorded before any implementation, or flagged.

00:05:34.39 --> 00:05:39.76
Each iteration shows against the cap, and a blocked increment lists what still

00:05:39.76 --> 00:05:40.17
fails.

00:05:40.17 --> 00:05:45.20
Required checks arrive by signed webhook or by poll, and turn green live.

00:05:45.20 --> 00:05:48.16
And the increment squash-merges on its own.

00:05:48.16 --> 00:05:50.09
Next comes journey fourteen.
